WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.934
Welcome to Survive Drive. Set a trip, compare candidate routes, inspect the evidence, and choose for yourself. The app organizes information; it does not declare a route safe. This tour shows the controls and why they exist.

2
00:00:15.634 --> 00:00:42.106
Most of this machinery exists to keep AVM lookups economical. A cell can be shared by several routes, and a saved value can be reused without a new RentCast request. At one cent per new lookup, ten million uncached lookups would total one hundred thousand dollars. A nationwide prefill could reach or exceed that scale, depending on grid density. SD focuses on the cells relevant to your trip instead.

3
00:00:42.806 --> 00:01:04.288
Enter coordinates or place the start and destination on the map. You can name each point, choose faster travel or a balance of time and distance, and set road preferences such as highways and tolls. These preferences guide Valhalla; avoid does not guarantee that a road type will disappear. This version does not use live traffic.

4
00:01:04.988 --> 00:01:29.617
Escape Gates offer possible approaches near both ends of the trip. Adjust the ring and gate settings, then select GO to find road suggestions. Review the gates before searching. With sixteen gates at each end, including the no-gate cases, the full cross product is seventeen by seventeen, or two hundred eighty-nine Valhalla requests. The exact batch is shown before the search.

5
00:01:30.317 --> 00:01:50.891
The route budget shows the prepared Valhalla requests and provider-attempt count. When the local Valhalla service is ready, the prepared batch starts automatically within that limit. A failed request stops the workflow for review. There is no automatic Valhalla retry. The route search does not spend AVM credits.

6
00:01:51.591 --> 00:02:15.664
Evaluate first maps eligible route cells and checks saved AVM values. Round zero ranks with that known evidence before asking for new paid lookups. Its default cutoff deactivates thirty-five percent of the nominal candidates, while routes with less than twenty percent known coverage are protected. Unknown cells remain unknown; they are not treated as low values.

7
00:02:16.364 --> 00:02:42.047
Each candidate shows its rank, known coverage, and unknown cells. INCLUDED means eligible for the next evidence round; EXCLUDED candidates remain visible and can return after reranking. The Red cutoff classifies known cells by price per square foot. This is a geographic value signal, not a measured safety rating. Highway runs and attached ramps are left out of AVM evaluation.

8
00:02:42.747 --> 00:03:07.862
For more evidence, choose the number of new cells per INCLUDED route. SD checks the shared cache and removes duplicate cells across routes. Calculate exact One Round lookups to see the final new-call count before you select Run. Each new lookup is counted as one credit at one cent in the current cost display. Failed attempts count, and paid provider calls are not retried automatically.

9
00:03:08.562 --> 00:03:28.293
Set the Red threshold for ranking and the upper map-color bound for visual contrast. Changing these settings reclassifies or recolors saved evidence without buying new values. Colored cells describe estimated property value around a route, not danger or safety. A missing AVM value still means unknown.

10
00:03:28.993 --> 00:03:48.212
Open Street View from a map point and check when the imagery was taken. Record what you notice separately from the AVM signal. Then compare route trade-offs and choose a candidate. Export the session or decision if you want a copy. You remain in control of the route and every new evidence round.
